|~ `::quote`, the kind of code held as a value, and what is declared
   under it: its constructor and its refusals, the refusal of text read
   as code and the facts it carries, and the verbs that read a quote. A
   quote is also the vector of its steps, so the verbs of vectors read
   it as one, and a quote under a tag, a link or a snippet of a page,
   reads them through the walk of its tags.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence]. ~|

:quote
  |~~ A quote is code held as a value, `~(add 1 | mul 2)`: steps that
      run only when something runs them. A verb that runs code later
      takes a quote, `map ~(mul 2)`, `filter ~(gt 1)`, `sort /name`,
      and `apply` runs one on a subject, `5 | apply ~(add 1)` answering
      `6`. A quote written in the step that hands it to a verb reads the
      names of that step wherever the verb runs it,
      `:k 10 | [1 2] | map ~(add k)` answering `[11 12]`; one held as
      data, in a name, a field or a page, runs in a lane of its own and
      reads no value the reader named.

      It is written `~(…)` around a pipeline, and one name drops the
      parentheses, `~count`. A verb that runs code takes a path bare,
      `sort /name`, so a quote of a path alone is refused where it is
      written; one built, by `parse` or by `tag ::quote`, answers the
      path and prints under its tag, `::quote[/name]`. A
      quote is the vector of its steps under `::quote`,
      `~(add 1 | mul 2) | count` answering `2`, and each step reads as
      data: a literal as itself, and a step
      that computes as a record, `~(add 1) | first` answering
      `::call{:name :add :modifiers [1]}`. `parse` turns text into a
      quote, `toString` a quote into the text of its code, and JSON
      writes a quote as its print.

      Because code is a value, a page holds links and snippets that run,
      the laws of a name are quotes that answer true, and the trace of a
      failure holds the steps it skipped, a quote that runs again as held
      code runs.
      ::link~(::call | doc) ::link~(::any/apply | doc)
      ::link~(::call/ValueWithheldError | doc) ::link~(::qlang/code | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts
        |~~ A vector whose every element is a step, built into the quote.
        ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::quote` builds code from the vector of its steps; a payload
            of another kind is refused, a vector under another tag among
            them, which `payload` opens.
            ::link~(::quote | doc)
        ~~|
    | throws :ElementNotStepError
        |~~ Every element of a quote is a step: a literal, a path, a
            quote, a vector, a set or a map of steps, or a step as data,
            `::call`, `::bind` and their kin. A value with no reading as
            code, a value under a tag of another kind among them and the
            quote of a path alone, which a step writes bare, is refused at
            the index it stands at.
            ::link~(::quote | doc)
        ~~|
    | throws :StepNotCommandError
        |~~ A step after the first is a command, a call, a declaration or
            a group, and a value stands at the head, where it is the
            subject, after a declaration and after `!|`, where it is the
            recovery: the step at `:index` is a value, and `:message` names
            the verb that takes it, `get` for a path and a literal, `tag`
            for a tag and `apply` for a quote. A value a step answers is
            built by a verb, `get [1 2]`, and a value under a tag by `tag`.
            ::link~(::quote | doc) ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)
        ~~|
    | carries :StepNotCommandError/message
        |~~ What the rule says of the value, the verb that takes it. ~~|
        ::string
    | throws :LiteralComputesError
        |~~ A literal computes nothing: the step at `:index` is a literal
            that holds a name or a group, which the shape `get` builds
            computes, and `:message` writes that template, its parts
            computed written as quotes, `get [~(count)]`.
            ::link~(::quote | doc) ::link~(::any/get | doc)
        ~~|
    | carries :LiteralComputesError/message
        |~~ What the rule says of the literal, the template `get` builds it
            from.
        ~~|
        ::string
    | throws :ParseError
        |~~ The source does not read as qlang: `:location` names where the
            reading stopped, its line and column read by `line` and
            `column` of its `start`, `:message` names the habit a form of
            another language wrote, and where none does `:expected` lists
            what could stand there and a source cut short names the bracket
            it left open.
            ::link~(::quote | doc) ::link~(::span | doc)
        ~~|
    | carries :ParseError/source
        |~~ The line of the text where the reading stopped. ~~|
        ::string
    | carries :ParseError/message
        |~~ What the grammar says where the reading stopped, a bracket
            left open among it.
        ~~|
        ::string
    | carries :ParseError/expected
        |~~ The continuations the reading could take where it stopped, each
            the text of a literal or a part of the grammar named as a
            keyword, `:name`, `:whitespace`, `:end-of-source`, beside
            what it met as `:found`.
        ~~|
        ::vec
    | carries :ParseError/found
        |~~ What the reading met where it stopped, none at the end of the
            source.
        ~~|
        ::string
    | carries :ParseError/location
        |~~ The span of the text where the reading stopped. ~~|
        ::span
    | carries :ParseError/origin
        |~~ Where the text came from, the source, the cell or the verb that
            read it.
        ~~|
        ::keyword
    | proves :countsItsSteps
        |~~ A quote of three steps counts three. ~~|
        ~(~(1 | add 1 | mul 2) | count | eq 3)
    | proves :printsADeclarationALine
        |~~ A quote of declarations alone prints a declaration a line. ~~|
        ~(~(:a 1 | :b 2) | toString | eq ":a 1\n| :b 2")
    | proves :equalsItsCopy
        |~~ Two quotes of one code are equal. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | eq ~(add 1))
    | proves :printsAPathAloneUnderItsTag
        |~~ A quote of a path alone, built, prints under its tag. ~~|
        ~("/name" | parse | print | eq "::quote[/name]")
    | proves :answersThePathItHoldsAlone
        |~~ A quote of a path alone answers the path, and reads nothing. ~~|
        ~({:name 1} | apply ("/name" | parse) | eq /name)
    | proves :buildsBackFromItsSteps
        |~~ The steps of a quote build it back. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | payload | tag ::quote | eq ~(add 1 | mul 2))
    | proves :filtersItsSteps
        |~~ A quote filters as the vector of its steps. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | filter ~(get /name | eq :mul) | toString | eq "mul 2")
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A map payload is refused. ~~|
        ~(::quote{:not :steps} !| get [~(tag) /actualTag] | eq [::quote/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::map])
    | proves :ElementNotStepError/refusesAValueWithNoReading
        |~~ A value with no reading as a step is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~([::Box[1]] | tag ::quote !| get [~(tag) /index] | eq [::quote/ElementNotStepError 0])
    | proves :ElementNotStepError/refusesTheQuoteOfAPathAlone
        |~~ The quote of a path alone, which a step writes bare, is refused
            at its index.
        ~~|
        ~(get [1 ~("/name" | parse)] | tag ::quote !| get [~(tag) /index] | eq [::quote/ElementNotStepError 1])
    | proves :StepNotCommandError/refusesAValueAfterTheHead
        |~~ A value after the first step is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~([[1] [2]] | tag ::quote !| get [~(tag) /index] | eq [::quote/StepNotCommandError 1])
    | proves :StepNotCommandError/namesTheVerbThatTakesThePath
        |~~ A path after the first step names the verb that reads it. ~~|
        ~([1 /a] | tag ::quote !| get /message | contains "write `get /a`")
    | proves :StepNotCommandError/keepsAValueAtTheHead
        |~~ A value at the head is the subject the steps after it run on.
        ~~|
        ~(get [[1 2] ~(~(count) | first)] | tag ::quote | apply it | eq 2)
    | proves :StepNotCommandError/keepsAValueAfterADeclaration
        |~~ A value after a declaration is the subject the steps after it
            run on.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~(~(:x 1) | first) 5] | tag ::quote | apply it | eq 5)
    | proves :LiteralComputesError/refusesANameInAVector
        |~~ A vector that holds a name is refused, naming the template
            `get` builds.
        ~~|
        ~([[::call{:name :count}]] | tag ::quote !| get [~(tag) /message] | eq [::quote/LiteralComputesError "a literal computes nothing: a shape computed is built by `get`, its parts paths and quotes, write `get [~(count)]`"])
    | proves :ParseError/refusesASourceCutShort
        |~~ A source cut short after a pipe is refused. ~~|
        ~("42 |" | parse !| tag | eq ::quote/ParseError)
    | proves :ParseError/namesTheLineAndTheColumnWhereTheReadingStopped
        |~~ The start of `:location` reads the line and the column where the
            reading stopped, under the line `:source` holds.
        ~~|
        ~("[1 2" | parse !| get [/source ~(get /location | start | get [~(line) ~(column)])] | eq ["[1 2" [1 5]])
    | proves :ParseError/namesTheContinuationsInTheWordsOfTheLanguage
        |~~ A continuation is the text of a literal or a part of the grammar
            named in the words of the language.
        ~~|
        ~(":" | parse !| get /expected | eq ["\"" :name])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:quote/toString
  |~~ Answers the text of the quote's code, a string, its steps without
      the brackets around them, `~(add 1 | mul 2) | toString` answering
      `"add 1 | mul 2"`, which `parse` reads back as the quote, so a quote
      turned into text and back is itself, `~(add 1) | toString | parse`
      answering `~(add 1)`. The steps are written in the one spelling the
      printer writes, so the spaces and the line breaks the quote was
      written with are not kept, `"5 |  mul   2" | parse | toString`
      answering `"5 | mul 2"`. A link or a snippet out of a page answers
      the query it holds, without its tags, and the empty quote the empty
      string; `print` writes the quote with its brackets.
      ::link~(::string/parse | doc) ::link~(::any/print | doc)
      ::link~(::quote/open | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The text the quote reads back from. ~~| ::string
    | proves :writesTheTextOfItsCode
        |~~ A quote answers the text of its code. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | toString | eq "add 1")
    | proves :readsBackAsTheQuote
        |~~ The text parsed again is the quote. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1 | mul 2) | :q it | q | toString | parse | eq q)
    | proves :writesOneSpelling
        |~~ The spaces the quote was written with are not kept. ~~|
        ~("5 |  mul   2" | parse | toString | eq "5 | mul 2")
    | proves :leavesItsBracketsToPrint
        |~~ `print` writes the quote with its brackets. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | get [~(toString) ~(print)] | eq ["add 1" "~(add 1)"])
    | proves :leavesTheTagsOfALink
        |~~ A link answers the query it holds, without its tags. ~~|
        ~(::link~(1 | add 1) | toString | eq "1 | add 1")
    | proves :answersNothingForTheEmptyQuote
        |~~ The empty quote writes the empty string. ~~|
        ~(~() | toString | eq "")
    | impl ::builtin{}

:quote/toVec
  |~~ Answers the vector of the quote's steps, the payload beneath its
      tag, `~(add 1 | mul 2) | toVec | count` answering `2`, as `toVec`
      of a set answers its elements.
      ::link~(::set/toVec | doc) ::link~(::any/payload | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The vector of the steps. ~~| ::vec
    | proves :answersTheVectorOfItsSteps
        |~~ A quote answers the vector of its steps, under a tag of its own
            as well.
        ~~|
        ~([~(add 1 | mul 2) ::Box~(add 1)] | map ~(toVec | get [~(tag) ~(count)]) | eq [[::vec 2] [::vec 1]])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:quote/open
  |~~ Answers what the query the quote holds answers, run beneath
      whatever tags stand over it, `~(1 | add 1) | open` answering `2`:
      a link opens to the page it names, so `links | map ~open` reads every
      page a page names in one query, and a snippet to the answer it
      shows. The query is code held as data and runs in a lane of its
      own: it reads the verbs of the tree and the names it binds, which
      stay inside it, and no value the reader named,
      `:k 5 | ~(k) | open` refused under `k`. It runs from null, not from the
      subject of `open`: a query that reads a subject, `~(add 1)`, is
      refused on null, and a quote meant for a subject runs against it
      by `apply`, `5 | apply ~(add 1)`. Text is read as a quote first by
      `parse`, `"1 | add 1" | parse | open`.
      ::link~(::any/apply | doc) ::link~(::doc/links | doc)
      ::link~(::doc/snippets | doc) ::link~(::qlang/code | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ What the query answers, a value of any kind. ~~| ::any
    | throws :SubjectNotQuoteError
        |~~ `open` runs a quote, `~(1 | add 1) | open`: text is read as a
            quote first by `parse`, `"1 | add 1" | parse | open`, and the
            links of a page are read by `links`,
            `::vec | doc | links | map ~open`.
        ~~|
    | proves :runsALink
        |~~ A link opens to the answer of its query. ~~|
        ~(|~~ ::link~(1 | add 1) ~~| | links | first | open | eq 2)
    | proves :runsASnippet
        |~~ A snippet opens to what it shows. ~~|
        ~(|~~ ::snippet~([3 1 2] | sort) ~~| | snippets | first | open | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :withholdsAValueOfTheReader
        |~~ A value the reader named is withheld from the query. ~~|
        ~(:k 5 | ~(k) | open !| tag | eq ::call/ValueWithheldError)
    | proves :keepsItsNamesInside
        |~~ A name the query binds stays inside it. ~~|
        ~(:k 5 | ~(:k 1) | open | k | eq 5)
    | proves :runsFromNull
        |~~ The query runs from null. ~~|
        ~(~(tag) | open | eq ::null)
    | proves :runsTextReadByParse
        |~~ Text read as a quote by `parse` opens. ~~|
        ~("1 | add 1" | parse | open | eq 2)
    | proves :SubjectNotQuoteError/refusesAString
        |~~ Text is no quote until `parse` reads it. ~~|
        ~("1 | add 1" | open !| get [~(tag) /actualTag] | eq [::quote/open/SubjectNotQuoteError ::string])
    | proves :refusesAQueryThatReadsASubject
        |~~ A query that reads a subject is refused on null. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | open !| tag | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

